<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niš</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Naissus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Niš is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on the Nišava river, one of the oldest cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. As Roman Naissus it was the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>. Taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in the late 14th century, it saw a Christian victory in 1443 during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>'s Long Campaign, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> deserted the Ottoman army. Serbia gained the city in 1878.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nis.jpg" alt="Niš" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manojlovic80 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ниш_34.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The ancient name Naissus is probably derived from an older form, *Nāviskos, from the Celtic name of the Nišava river, meaning trough valley. The first settlement may have been founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> at the end of the 3rd century BCE, though little archaeological evidence survives from before the Roman period. The road running south down the Morava valley from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> forks at Niš: one line leads to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, the other eastward to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the Roman conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, between 168 and 75 BCE, the city served as a base of operations. It is first mentioned in Roman documents near the beginning of the 2nd century CE and appears in the Geography of Ptolemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. Several Roman emperors were born in Naissus or used it as a residence, among them Constantius III, Constans, Vetranio, Julian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-i">Justin I</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius Gothicus</a> decisively defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> at the Battle of Naissus. Its role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> earned it the nickname Imperial City.</p><p>In 805 Bulgarian Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a> took the town and its surroundings, and in the 11th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> reclaimed them. The Ottoman Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> took the city in 1385 after a siege of 25 days. It returned to Serbian rule in 1443 but fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> again in 1448 and stayed under them for 241 years, as a seat of military and civil administration. The Austrian army seized it in 1689 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a> and the Ottomans regained it in 1690. They retook it again in 1737 without resistance, after an Austrian occupation that year. The existing fortress was built in 1719–1723 on the site of Roman, Byzantine and medieval fortifications.</p><p>After liberation in 1878 the city became part of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. A library and the first hotel opened in 1879, a grammar school in 1878 and the City Hall was built in 1882–1887. From 1929 to 1941 Niš was the capital of the Morava Banovina of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. During the German occupation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Crveni Krst concentration camp operated here. About 30,000 people passed through it, and over 10,000 were shot on nearby Bubanj hill. On 12 February 1942, 147 prisoners staged a mass escape.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Niš is the third-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and the administrative centre of the Nišava District, with a city proper of fewer than two hundred thousand people. It is the seat of the University of Niš and has an international airport named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>. Its industries include tobacco, electronics and mechanical engineering.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niš” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
